### Treatment with a FAK inhibitor leads to elevated parasite loads in the brain parenchyma

To determine the impact of the BBB integrity on parenchymal parasite loads, mice were treated with a second-generation inhibitor (defactinib, VS) ([Kanteti et al., 2018](#bib38); [Gerber et al., 2020](#bib28)) of the intercellular junction regulator FAK. VS treatment led to significantly elevated parasite loads in the brain for both high-dose (hd) and low-dose (ld) *T. gondii* challenges ([Figure 8a, b and c](#fig8), [Figure 8—figure supplement 1a](#fig8s1)), with an impact on parasite loads in peripheral organs. Detected IFN-γ/IL-12 responses in brain homogenate were in similar range as untreated, with variability between mice ([Figure 8—figure supplement 1b, c](#fig8s1)). Importantly, the number of parasites in the intravascular and perivascular space were significantly increased early during infection ([Figure 8d](#fig8)). However, at day eight post-inoculation, VS-treated mice exhibited a significant increase in the number of parasite foci in the intravascular, perivascular and parenchymal space, related to untreated mice ([Figure 8e](#fig8)). Moreover, the numbers and sizes of parasite foci were elevated ([Figure 8f and g](#fig8)), indicating earlier penetration to the parenchyma upon VS-treatment. Leakage of the vascular tracer EB to the parenchyma was significantly increased in VS-treated mice compared to non-treated mice ([Figure 8h and i](#fig8)), indicating increased permeability. Overall, we conclude that pharmacological inhibition of the vascular intercellular TJ regulator FAK increases BBB permeability and significantly elevates parasite loads in the brain parenchyma.

