### Conditional deletion of endothelial FAK in mice impacts the passage of *T. gondii* to the brain parenchyma

The impact of pharmacological FAK antagonism on parenchymal parasite loads motivated a deletion of endothelial FAK-encoding *Ptk2* in mice. To generate endothelial cell-specific deletion of *Ptk2* (ECPTK2 cKO), *Ptk2flox/flox* mice were crossed with *Cdh5cre+/-* mice harboring a tamoxifen-inducible Cre recombinase under the regulation of vascular endothelial cadherin promoter. To confirm FAK deletion in genotyped mice ([Figure 9—figure supplement 1a](#fig9s1)), fractions enriched in brain endothelial cells (MBECs) were isolated ([Ross et al., 2019](#bib58)). Western blotting showed a significant reduction in total FAK protein expression in MBEC-enriched preparations from ECPTK2 cKO mice ([Figure 9a](#fig9)), with undetectable changes of FAK expression in bone-marrow-derived cells (BMDCs) ([Figure 9—figure supplement 1b, c](#fig9s1)). Tamoxifen-induced FAK deletion non-significantly impacted total parasite loads in brain tissue and in peripheral organs ([Figure 9b](#fig9)), and BBB impermeability to EB was maintained ([Figure 9c](#fig9)), consistent with the reported absence of detectable systemic derangement of vascular permeability ([Lee et al., 2010](#bib44); [Weis et al., 2008](#bib72)). Importantly, a detailed analysis of parasite foci in the brain parenchyma ([Figure 9d](#fig9)) detected elevated numbers ([Figure 9e](#fig9)) and size ([Figure 9f](#fig9)) of parasite foci in ECPTK2 cKO mice, related to ECPTK2 WT mice. Tamoxifen-treated cre control mice exhibited non-significant differences in peripheral parasite loads and parenchymal parasite foci numbers ([Figure 9—figure supplement 1d-f](#fig9s1)). The data is consistent with a BBB dysregulative, but non-disruptive, effect of FAK deletion on MBECs ([Ross et al., 2019](#bib58)). We conclude that endothelial FAK deletion impacts the passage of *T. gondii* to the brain parenchyma and yields elevated numbers of parasite foci.

